## Why This Is Asked

Quicksort with many duplicates can degrade; 3-way partitioning reduces swaps and comparisons.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)
- In-place iterative sorting (no recursion)
- Median-of-three pivot to avoid degeneracy
- Space: O(log n) for manual stack

## Code Example

```javascript
function quicksort3WayIterative(arr) {
  const stack = [];
  stack.push([0, arr.length - 1]);
  while (stack.length) {
    const [l, r] = stack.pop();
    if (l >= r) continue;
    const m = l + Math.floor((r - l) / 2);
    // median-of-three to pick pivot
    if (arr[l] > arr[m]) [arr[l], arr[m]] = [arr[m], arr[l]];
    if (arr[l] > arr[r]) [arr[l], arr[r]] = [arr[r], arr[l]];
    if (arr[m] > arr[r]) [arr[m], arr[r]] = [arr[r], arr[m]];
    const pivot = arr[m];
    // Dutch National Flag partition
    let i = l, lt = l, gt = r;
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (arr[i] < pivot) { [arr[lt], arr[i]] = [arr[i], arr[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
      else if (arr[i] > pivot) { [arr[i], arr[gt]] = [arr[gt], arr[i]]; gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    stack.push([l, lt - 1]);
    stack.push([gt + 1, r]);
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify stability with duplicates in this approach?
- How would you adapt to 64-bit integers and different memory constraints?

Why This Is Asked\n\nThis question probes understanding of practical sorting trade-offs beyond textbook asymptotics, including cache locality, in-place constraints, and robust pivot strategies. It invites discussion of 3-way partitioning to handle duplicates, median-of-three to curb worst-case behavior, and a small insertion sort for tiny partitions.\n\n## Key Concepts\n- 3-way partitioning in quicksort\n- median-of-three pivot selection\n- insertion sort threshold tuning\n- in-place algorithms and cache efficiency\n- iterative vs recursive implementations\n\n## Code Example\n```javascript
function sortInPlace(arr){
  const TH = 16;
  const swap=(a,i,j)=>{const t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;};
  const medianOfThree=(a,i,j,k)=>{let x=[a[i],i],[a[j],j],[a[k],k].sort((p,q)=>p[0]-q[0]); return a[x[1]];};
  // iterative 3-way quicksort skeleton
  const stack=[[0,arr.length-1]];
  while(stack.length){
    const [lo,hi]=stack.pop();
    if(hi-lo+1<=TH){ // insertion sort for small partitions
      for(let i=lo+1;i<=hi;i++){
        let t=arr[i], j=i-1;
        while(j>=lo && arr[j]>t){ arr[j+1]=arr[j]; j-- }
        arr[j+1]=t;
      }
      continue;
    }
    // median-of-three pivot
    const mid=(lo+hi)>>1;
    const pivot= medianOfThree(arr, lo, mid, hi);
    // 3-way partition
    let lt=lo, i=lo, gt=hi;
    while(i<=gt){
      if(arr[i]<pivot) swap(arr, lt++, i++);
      else if(arr[i]>pivot) swap(arr, i, gt--);
      else i++;
    }
    stack.push([lo, lt-1]);
    stack.push([gt+1, hi]);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine weighted shortest paths with path counting via a DAG, a common real-world need in routing and logistics.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra for non-negative weights
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological DP to count paths
- Time/space trade-offs and modular arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode outline for counting shortest paths
function countShortestPaths(graph, s, t) {
  const dist = dijkstra(graph, s);
  const dag = buildShortestPathDag(graph, dist);
  const order = topologicalSort(dag);
  const ways = new Array(graph.n).fill(0);
  ways[s] = 1;
  for (const u of order) {
    for (const [v] of dag.adj[u]) {
      ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % 1000000007;
    }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t], count: ways[t] };
}
```

