## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of Trie data structures, deduplication strategies, and efficient prefix queries—essential skills for building autocomplete systems.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node subtree counts for O(1) prefix counting
- Deduplication via global set to maintain word uniqueness
- Prefix-based retrieval with controlled result limits
- Lexicographic ordering through systematic child traversal

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.isWord = false;
    this.passCount = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.wordSet = new Set();
  }

  insert(word) {
    if (this.wordSet.has(word)) return;
    this.wordSet.add(word);
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) {
        node.next.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.next.get(char);
      node.passCount++;
    }
    node.isWord = true;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return 0;
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    return node.passCount;
  }

  listWithPrefix(prefix, limit) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    const result = [];
    this.dfs(node, prefix, result, limit);
    return result;
  }

  dfs(node, current, result, limit) {
    if (result.length >= limit) return;
    if (node.isWord) {
      result.push(current);
    }
    const sortedKeys = Array.from(node.next.keys()).sort();
    for (const char of sortedKeys) {
      this.dfs(node.next.get(char), current + char, result, limit);
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of in-place sorting algorithms, particularly the challenging problem of merging sorted subarrays without auxiliary space. It distinguishes candidates who understand the theoretical difference between standard merge sort (O(n) space) and true in-place variants, and reveals familiarity with classical algorithms like the Ford-Johnson merge sort or rotation-based merging.

## Key Concepts

- **In-place merge**: Merging two sorted subarrays within the original array without allocating a separate buffer
- **Stability**: Preserving the relative order of equal elements, critical for multi-key sorting
- **Bottom-up merge sort**: Iterative approach that starts with singleton runs and doubles the merge width each pass
- **Rotation-based merging**: Using cyclic block swaps to interleave elements from two sorted segments
- **Time/space complexities**: O(n log n) average time, O(n²) worst-case due to rotation cost; O(1) extra space

## Code Example

```javascript
function rotate(arr, l, m, r) {
  // Rotates arr[l..m-1] with arr[m..r-1] in-place
  // Uses block-swap: hold arr[l], shift left block right by one, repeat cyclically
}

function inPlaceMerge(arr, l, m, r) {
  // Merge sorted runs [l, m) and [m, r) in-place
  // Find the smaller run, rotate it into position
}

function inPlaceMergeSort(arr) {
  // Bottom-up: start with width=1, double each iteration
  for (let w = 1; w < arr.length; w *= 2) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += 2 * w) {
      inPlaceMerge(arr, i, Math.min(i + w, arr.length), Math.min(i + 2 * w, arr.length));
    }
  }
}
```

**In-place example**: arr = [1, 3, 5 | 2, 4, 6], merging runs [1,3,5] and [2,4,6] rotates to [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6].

